{% endif -%}
Candela Pro is the equity-research assistant of [Candela](https://github.com/UmitVice/candela): **Gemma 4 12B on Google's quantization-aware-trained (QAT) int4 weights plus a QLoRA adapter**, trained locally on a laptop. The int4 base stays frozen exactly as Google trained it and the adapter is never merged, so 4-bit is the default with the base's QAT quality intact. This repository holds only the {{ artifact }} ({{ "%.1f"|format(train.trainable / 1e6) }} M parameters); the base model is downloaded separately.

- Base: [`{{ base_model }}`](https://huggingface.co/{{ base_model }}){% if library == "gguf" %} · GGUF base: [`{{ base_gguf }}`](https://huggingface.co/{{ base_gguf }}){% endif %}
- Companion repos: [`{{ repos.adapter }}`](https://huggingface.co/{{ repos.adapter }}) (MLX) · [`{{ repos.gguf }}`](https://huggingface.co/{{ repos.gguf }}) (GGUF LoRA) · dataset [`{{ repos.dataset }}`](https://huggingface.co/datasets/{{ repos.dataset }})
- Code, data pipeline and evaluation harness: https://github.com/UmitVice/candela (`ml/`), run `{{ run_id }}`

## Intended use

An educational equity-research assistant that answers questions about listed companies by **calling market tools** (`search_symbols`, `get_quote`, `get_price_history`, `get_technical_indicators`, `get_fundamentals`, `get_forecast`; schemas in [`contracts/tools.json`](https://github.com/UmitVice/candela/blob/main/contracts/tools.json)) and writing grounded Markdown answers with Snapshot, Trend & Momentum, Fundamentals, Outlook & Risks and Bottom line sections. It is meant to be served with the Candela system prompt ([`contracts/system-prompt.md`](https://github.com/UmitVice/candela/blob/main/contracts/system-prompt.md)) and a tool executor that returns real data. Thinking mode stays available but the adapter was trained with thinking off.

**Not financial advice.** The model gives educational analysis only; it declines personalized buy/sell/hold advice and must not be used to make investment decisions.

## Usage

{% if library == "gguf" -%}
Download the QAT Q4 GGUF base (for example from [`{{ base_gguf }}`](https://huggingface.co/{{ base_gguf }})) and this LoRA, then run llama.cpp:

```bash
llama-server -m gemma-4-12b-it-qat-q4_0.gguf --lora {{ files[0] }} --jinja -c 8192
```

The adapter was converted from the MLX adapter (MLX → PEFT → `convert_lora_to_gguf.py` at llama.cpp `b9960`) and checked by loading it with `llama-server --lora` on the QAT Q4 GGUF (answers with LoRA scale 1 differ from scale 0). Tools are passed through the OpenAI-compatible `tools` field; llama.cpp renders them with Gemma 4's chat template.
{%- else -%}
The MLX base is Google's QAT checkpoint converted to MLX affine int4 with group size 32:

```bash
mlx_lm.convert --hf-path {{ base_model }} --mlx-path gemma-4-12b-it-qat-mlx-q4 -q --q-bits 4 --q-group-size 32
hf download {{ repos.adapter }} --local-dir candela-pro-adapter
mlx_lm.generate --model gemma-4-12b-it-qat-mlx-q4 --adapter-path candela-pro-adapter \
  --prompt "What does an RSI of 75 usually indicate?" --max-tokens 200
```

```python
from mlx_lm import load, generate

model, tokenizer = load("gemma-4-12b-it-qat-mlx-q4", adapter_path="candela-pro-adapter")
messages = [{"role": "system", "content": "<Candela system prompt>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Analyze NVDA"}]
prompt = tokenizer.apply_chat_template(messages, tools=TOOLS, add_generation_prompt=True, enable_thinking=False)
print(generate(model, tokenizer, prompt=prompt, max_tokens=512))
```

Tool calls use Gemma 4's native syntax (`<|tool_call>call:get_quote{symbol:<|"|>NVDA<|"|>}<tool_call|>`); mlx-lm's `gemma4` tool parser reads them. Inference needs about 8 GB of unified memory.
{%- endif %}

